Aristotle's phantasia functions as a mediating faculty that discloses absent objects by making them present to consciousness without requiring their actual sensory presence, thereby establishing a mode of access to the world that transcends immediate perception. Heidegger's interpretation captures this distinctive operation when he characterizes Aristotelian thinking as involving φαντασία, the "making-present-to-itself" of the world, in which what is made present is not actually there, but instead is, say, in memory. Indeed, Aristotle's theory of phantasia attempts to deal with a large range of problems concerning how consciousness accesses what is not immediately given in sensation, thus positioning this faculty as central to understanding cognitive processes that extend beyond direct perceptual encounter. Specifically, the phantasma creates a certain impression that differs from the eikōn or likeness, suggesting that phantasia produces appearances rather than mere copies of sensory data, thereby establishing a mode of presentation irreducible to mimetic reproduction. These textual elements consequently demonstrate that phantasia operates as a distinctive cognitive capacity that bridges the gap between sensory absence and mental presence, for by making-present-to-itself what is not actually there, phantasia enables thought to operate on objects beyond immediate sensory contact, establishing a fundamental structure of disclosure that makes absent entities accessible to consciousness. This mediating function subsequently positions phantasia as more than mere image-production—it is a faculty that opens up a domain of appearances independent of direct perception, hence constituting a necessary condition for cognitive activity that extends beyond the spatiotemporal constraints of immediate sensory experience.

While Aristotle's account remains within a faculty psychology framework, the structural insight about mediated presence anticipates phenomenological concerns that reach their fullest articulation in Heidegger's ontological reformulation of disclosure. Heidegger's Erschlossenheit constitutes the fundamental existential structure through which Dasein's 'there' (Da) is opened up, establishing disclosedness as more primordial than any epistemological relation and as constitutive of Dasein's very Being rather than a property it possesses. Heidegger emphasizes that "if it lacks its 'there', it is not factically the entity which is essentially Dasein; indeed, it is not this entity at all," indicating that disclosedness is constitutive of Dasein's Being rather than an attribute that might contingently belong to it. Furthermore, the analysis shows that "the being-there of the human being was explicated as being-in-the-world, in such a way that λόγος came forth as the basic character of this being, determined as being-in," connecting disclosure to logos as fundamental structure. These passages establish that Erschlossenheit is not an epistemological capacity that Dasein possesses but rather the ontological structure that Dasein is, whereby the 'there' (Da) of Dasein refers to the opened-up space of intelligibility within which entities can show themselves. Without this disclosedness, Dasein would not be the kind of entity it essentially is, and this represents a radical departure from traditional theories of knowledge or mental representation, positioning disclosure as the condition for any possible relation to entities rather than as one cognitive function among others.

The structural parallels between phantasia and Erschlossenheit become most evident when we recognize that both function as intermediary structures enabling access to what is not immediately present through a mode of disclosure that resists reduction to either pure receptivity or pure spontaneity. Phantasia operates as 'making-present-to-itself' of what 'is not actually there,' establishing a mode of presence-in-absence that mediates between sensation and thought, while Heidegger's analysis demonstrates that being-in-the-world involves λόγος as 'the basic character' that structures disclosure, indicating a mediating interpretive dimension that similarly transcends the dichotomy between passive reception and active construction. Both frameworks address how 'intentionality is required, one which makes error possible; and this requires its content to be explained in a different way,' pointing to a shared concern with non-immediate, mediated access to entities that cannot be adequately captured by models of direct sensory apprehension or purely conceptual determination. This claim reflects Heidegger's mature position in Being and Time, though his interpretation of Aristotle in the 1920s lectures shows the developmental trajectory of these ideas, revealing how his engagement with phantasia's mediating structure informed his articulation of Erschlossenheit as the ontological condition for Dasein's access to entities through modes of disclosure that are neither purely given nor purely constructed.

The structural parallel emerges in how both concepts address the fundamental problem of mediated disclosure, though this parallel remains structural rather than substantive, as both address similar phenomenological problems while operating within fundamentally different ontological frameworks. Phantasia makes absent sensory objects available to thought by producing appearances that are neither mere sensations nor pure concepts, establishing a mediating capacity that enables cognitive access to what is not immediately present through sensory channels. Erschlossenheit, in a structurally analogous manner, opens up the world through an interpretive structure that is neither passive reception of data nor active construction of representations, constituting instead the ontological condition for Dasein's engagement with entities in their being. Both operate in an intermediary space where the world becomes accessible through a structure that enables both truth and error, indicating genuine intentional directedness rather than mechanical causation or arbitrary invention, thereby revealing that disclosure requires a mediating dimension that transcends the traditional epistemological dichotomy between receptivity and spontaneity. Hence, while Aristotle's phantasia functions within a hylomorphic psychology concerned with the soul's capacities for representing absent particulars, and Heidegger's Erschlossenheit operates within a fundamental ontology concerned with the being of Dasein as such, both frameworks converge on the recognition that access to entities demands a structured mediation that is simultaneously world-disclosing and error-permitting, pointing to a shared phenomenological insight that resists reduction to either empiricist or rationalist models of cognition.

Nevertheless, this convergence on the necessity of structured mediation masks a fundamental ontological divergence that separates Aristotelian faculty psychology from Heideggerian existential analytic, for Aristotle operates within a substance-ontology framework where phantasia functions as a capacity of the soul directed toward 'the τί ἤν εἶναι' which 'constitutes the particularity of beings,' thereby treating disclosure as a property possessed by an already-constituted entity defined by its essential nature. Heidegger, by contrast, rejects this entire framework by making disclosedness constitutive of Dasein's Being itself rather than a faculty it possesses, emphasizing that without its 'there,' Dasein 'is not factically the entity which is essentially Dasein; indeed, it is not this entity at all,' such that the structure of Erschlossenheit cannot be understood as an attribute inhering in a substance but must be grasped as the ontological condition for Dasein's existence as such. This rejection of pre-given faculties appears explicitly in Heidegger's insistence that 'Initially this conceptuality is not there in an original way; the possibilities of the conceptual are initially cut off by being-there,' indicating that existential structures emerge through Dasein's factical engagement rather than being antecedently available as psychological capacities. Subsequently, where Aristotle's hylomorphic psychology permits the analysis of phantasia as one faculty among others within a soul-body composite, Heidegger's fundamental ontology dissolves the very distinction between entity and capacity by locating disclosure in the existential constitution of Dasein as Being-in-the-world, thereby transforming what appears in Aristotle as a psychological question into an ontological problematic concerning the meaning of Being itself.

The divergence between Aristotelian phantasia and Heideggerian Erschlossenheit is ontological rather than merely terminological, marking a fundamental shift from faculty psychology to existential ontology. Aristotle's phantasia operates within a hylomorphic framework where the soul (psychē) possesses various faculties (dynameis) that actualize potentialities according to the substance's essential nature, such that phantasia constitutes one such faculty—a capacity that the soul has alongside perception, memory, and rational thought. Heidegger's Erschlossenheit, by contrast, is not something Dasein has but what Dasein is, whereby disclosedness cannot be understood as a property that could be added to or subtracted from an already-constituted subject but must be grasped as the very structure through which Dasein exists as the kind of being it is. This ontological reorientation dissolves the subject-predicate logic governing Aristotelian faculty psychology, replacing it with an existential analytic in which Being-in-the-world is not composed of a subject equipped with various capacities but is constituted as a unified phenomenon of disclosure. Despite this fundamental divergence, however, Heidegger's sustained engagement with Aristotle in his 1920s lectures suggests he saw his own project as radicalizing rather than simply rejecting Aristotelian insights, thereby indicating that the movement from faculty to existential structure represents a retrieval and transformation of Greek ontology rather than its wholesale abandonment.

Heidegger's phenomenological retrieval of Aristotle effects a radical transformation of disclosure by repositioning logos and alētheia as fundamental structures of Being-in-the-world rather than as epistemological mechanisms operating within a representational framework. His lectures demonstrate that "being-there of the human being was explicated as being-in-the-world, in such a way that λόγος came forth as the basic character of this being," thereby establishing logos not as a faculty of judgment or propositional assertion but as the constitutive articulation through which Dasein's existential situation becomes intelligible. This reinterpretation grounds disclosure in alētheia understood as uncoveredness rather than as the correctness of mental representations, a move Heidegger reinforces by emphasizing that in certain modes of disclosure "there is for uncovering no possibility of dissimulating, of mis-leading," suggesting that primordial access to beings bypasses the truth-as-correspondence model altogether. Moreover, Heidegger's etymological excavations reveal how Aristotelian categories themselves encode an ontology of presence: "Τοιόνδε, τοσόνδε, and so on: -δε proceeding from δή, 'manifestly there,' 'present'—ways of being-present, by which the presence of beings themselves is determined," indicating that what later tradition interpreted as logical predicates originally articulated modes of phenomenal manifestation. Through this hermeneutic strategy, Heidegger retrieves Aristotelian insights about phantasia and logos while simultaneously dismantling the substance-accident metaphysics and subject-object epistemology that had subsequently ossified around them, hence demonstrating that his existential analytic radicalizes rather than repudiates the Greek beginning of ontology.

This phenomenological retrieval (Wiederholung) constitutes a deliberate philosophical transformation rather than antiquarian scholarship, extracting Aristotelian insights about disclosure, presence, and uncoveredness from their embeddedness in faculty psychology and recasting them as existential structures fundamental to Dasein's way of being. By anchoring logos in Being-in-the-world and reinterpreting alētheia as the uncoveredness of entities rather than as a property of propositions, Heidegger systematically transforms Aristotelian concepts into constitutive elements of his existential analytic, thereby claiming to radicalize what remained implicit in Aristotle by liberating these insights from the metaphysical apparatus of substance, form, and faculty that had subsequently enclosed them. The question of whether this appropriation faithfully represents Aristotle's original intentions continues to generate scholarly controversy, with critics arguing that Heidegger projects existential structures backward onto texts that fundamentally presuppose the substance ontology he seeks to dismantle, while defenders maintain that his reading uncovers genuine phenomenological dimensions obscured by later metaphysical systematization. Nonetheless, the philosophical productivity of this hermeneutic engagement proves undeniable in the subsequent development of existential phenomenology, insofar as Heidegger's Aristotelian retrieval provides conceptual resources for articulating pre-theoretical disclosure, temporal ekstasis, and the priority of practical comportment over theoretical representation, thereby establishing a methodological paradigm that extends far beyond questions of historical fidelity to shape twentieth-century Continental philosophy's understanding of intentionality, worldhood, and interpretive understanding.

The divergence between Aristotle's representational model of phantasia, which produces mental images (phantasmata) that re-present absent objects, and Heidegger's non-representational account of world-disclosure reveals fundamentally different ontological commitments about how entities become accessible to understanding. Within Aristotle's framework, phantasia functions as a mediating faculty that produces phantasmata which "create a certain impression" distinct from direct likeness (eikōn), thereby suggesting a representational intermediary between mind and world that enables thought to operate upon absent objects through internally generated images. The interpretive tradition demonstrates that phantasia "attempts to deal with a large range of problems" concerning perception, memory, and conceptual thought, all within a framework where mental contents necessarily mediate cognitive access to external reality, establishing a subject-object division that subsequent epistemology would inherit and systematize. Heidegger's analysis, by contrast, emphasizes that disclosure operates through "being-in-the-world" as a unitary structure rather than through mental representations, with "λόγος" serving as "the basic character" of this being-in, thereby precluding any mediating layer of phantasmata between Dasein and the entities it encounters. This fundamental opposition between representational and non-representational models of access crystallizes the broader philosophical stakes of Heidegger's retrieval, insofar as his rejection of phantasia as a mediating faculty constitutes a systematic dismantling of the epistemological problematic that has dominated Western philosophy since Descartes, replacing questions of how internal representations correspond to external objects with an investigation of the prior conditions that make entities show up as intelligible within a disclosed world.

The representational versus non-representational divide marks a fundamental ontological difference that structures the entire philosophical distance between Aristotle's phantasia and Heidegger's Erschlossenheit, insofar as this divide determines not merely how cognition operates but what sort of being the cognitive agent is. Aristotle's phantasia operates by producing mental images that stand in for absent sensory objects, maintaining a distinction between the mental representation and the external reality it represents, thereby preserving a subject-object framework where the mind contains representations of an external world that exist as intermediaries between the perceiving subject and the perceived entities. Heidegger's Erschlossenheit, by contrast, rejects this entire framework by arguing that Dasein is always already in-the-world, not as a subject confronting objects through representational intermediaries, but as a being whose very existence is constituted by its openness to entities, such that disclosure is not mediated by mental representations but is the primordial structure through which entities show themselves in their intelligibility. Although some interpreters argue that Aristotle's phantasia is less straightforwardly representational than traditional readings suggest, attempting to locate in his account a more direct or non-mediating relationship between phantasmata and their objects, the textual evidence supports a representational interpretation in which phantasmata function as mental intermediaries that make absent objects cognitively available without collapsing the ontological distinction between the mental image and the external entity it represents. Hence the Aristotelian framework remains committed to a mediational structure that Heidegger's phenomenology systematically dismantles by locating intelligibility not in mental contents but in the disclosive structure of being-in-the-world itself.

